items/lem-cauchy-complete-and-archimedean-imply-mct.md:77:5.1 Since $\varepsilon > 0$ is invertible with $\varepsilon^{-1} > 0$, the Archimedean property supplies $j \ge 1$ with $(B - x_{k_0})\varepsilon^{-1} < j \cdot 1_F$, hence $B - x_{k_0} < (j \cdot 1_F)\varepsilon$ and $x_{k_j} \ge x_{k_0} + (j \cdot 1_F)\varepsilon > B$, contradicting the hypothesis that $B$ bounds every term of $(x_k)$. [step 4.1, L3, L6]
