items/cex-infinite-product-diverges-though-the-series-converges.md:96:1.1 For every $j$, $\iota(j+2) \ge \iota(2) = 2$, so $\sqrt{\iota(j+2)} \ge \sqrt 2 > 1$ and $|p_j| = 1/\sqrt{\iota(j+2)} \le 1/\sqrt 2 < 1$; hence every factor satisfies $0 < f_j \le 1 + 1/\sqrt 2 < 2$, and every $\Pi_m$ is positive. [given, L1, L2, L3, L6]
items/cex-infinite-product-diverges-though-the-series-converges.md:102:2.1 The sequence $\beta_j := 1/\sqrt{\iota(j+2)}$ is positive, nonincreasing and converges to $0$: monotonicity from $0 < \iota(j+2) < \iota(j+3)$ and strict increase of the square root, and convergence because, given a rational $\varepsilon > 0$, an $n \ge 1$ with $1/\iota(n) < \varepsilon^{2}$ gives $\iota(j+2) > (1/\varepsilon)^{2}$ and so $\beta_j < \varepsilon$ for every $j \ge n$. [step 1.1, L2, L3]
items/cex-infinite-product-diverges-though-the-series-converges.md:112:3.2 Combining, $P_i \le 1 - \bigl(2/\iota(4i+5)\bigr)\cdot\tfrac12 = 1 - 1/\iota(4i+5) \le 1 - q_i$, where $q_i := 1/\iota(4i+6)$, using $\iota(4i+5) < \iota(4i+6)$; and $0 < P_i$ by step 1.1, while $0 < q_i < 1$. [step 1.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, L3]
