items/thm-rearrangement-sums-lie-in-an-affine-subspace.md:93:1.4 Now let $n = 1$ and suppose $\sum x_k$ converges conditionally, so the real series $\sum_k (x_k)_0$ converges and $\sum_k|(x_k)_0|$ diverges. For $a \in \mathbb{R}^{1}$, $\langle a, x_k\rangle = a_0 (x_k)_0$ and $|\langle a,x_k\rangle| = |a_0|\,|(x_k)_0|$; if $a_0 \ne 0$ then convergence of $\sum_k |a_0||(x_k)_0|$ would give convergence of $\sum_k |(x_k)_0|$ after multiplying by the positive $1/|a_0|$, which is false, so $a \in \Gamma$ forces $a_0 = 0$; and $a = 0$ does lie in $\Gamma$. Hence $\Gamma = \{0\}$. [L1, L2, L9]
