items/thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.md:95:2.2 For $k \in \mathbb{N}$ put $X_k := \{\, x \in A \ : \ 0 < |x - c| < 1/(k+1) \ \text{ and } \ |f(x) - L| \ge \varepsilon_0 \,\}$. Each $X_k$ is nonempty, since $k + 1 \ge 1$ makes $1/(k+1)$ a positive real and step 1.2 applies to that radius. [step 1.2, L4, L6]
items/thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.md:113:- **Why $1/(k+1)$ and not $1/k$.** Sequences here are functions on $\mathbb{N}$ and $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so the index $k = 0$ occurs and $1/k$ would be undefined there. The same convention is used in [[lem-sequential-characterisation-of-closure-r]].

